# Host-platform build artifacts — must NOT leak into the Linux build context.
# Without this, `COPY . .` in apps/api/Dockerfile copies the host's own
# node_modules (e.g. macOS @esbuild/darwin-arm64) into the image, and
# `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` does not always replace already-present
# native optional-dependency binaries — causing esbuild/tsx to fail at
# runtime with "you installed esbuild for another platform" inside the
# Linux container. See docs/deployment-runbook.md staging validation notes.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# Root `dist/` is excluded. packages/*/dist AND apps/*/dist are
# intentionally NOT excluded (see apps/api/Dockerfile's comment on the build
# stage): tsc running against COPY'd (not host-native) source inside a Docker
# build was found unreliable — first for packages/schemas, packages/model-router
# and packages/prompt-engine on this developer's Docker Desktop (Production
# Step 2B), then confirmed to hit apps/api's own in-container build too, on a
# genuinely different environment (a GitHub Actions amd64 Linux runner,
# Production Step 8) — Step 2B's original theory that this was specific to
# "this Docker Desktop installation" was too narrow; the same class of bug
# can hit either platform. The pragmatic workaround, applied uniformly to
# all four buildable workspace packages now: ship each one's already-host-built
# dist/ in the image, build NOTHING with tsc inside the container. This means
# `pnpm run build` must have been run on the HOST for every workspace package
# before `docker compose -f docker-compose.staging.yml build` — see
# docs/staging-compose.md, docs/ci-stable-profile.md ("Production Step 8"),
# and docs/deployment-runbook.md.
/dist/
.next/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo

.git/
.env
.env.local
.env.production
.env.staging

uploads/
generated/
*.psd

*.log
logs/
coverage/
.nyc_output/
.test-pgdata/
